<urn:atm:triage:finding/RBP-F007>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "RBP-F007" ;
  triage:title "Model aliases are stringly typed: ModelAliases::latest(&str) -> Option<ModelId>, with_over" ;
  triage:description "Model aliases are stringly typed: ModelAliases::latest(&str) -> Option<ModelId>, with_override(&str, ..), default_model_alias() -> &'static str, and ModelDescriptor::released is a &'static str date. Because lookup is fallible for a built-in backend, the plan adds a provided model_id() whose unwrap_or_else(ModelId::fallback_for(self.id())) builds an Other(UnknownModel) holding a backend id string. That is not a model string, yet it would be logged as model_id. The only guard is test_backend_catalog_roundtrip. A non-catalog backend that does not override model_id() silently logs the catalog's model, which can be wrong after a [models] override. Recommendation: Use a non_exhaustive ModelAlias enum {Jev, Haiku, Luna} with FromStr (CONFIG_INVALID listing valid aliases). Then default_model_alias() and builtin().latest(alias) are infallible, and fallback_for and the unwrap_or_else path can be removed. Consider requiring model_id() on non-built-in backends. A typed release date or an ordering by table position avoids string-date comparison for 'newest'." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-a-refine" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "RBP" ;
  triage:severity "minor" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "file_only" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-11-07T22:47:49Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:A1-PLAN67 ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-11-07T23:55:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
